---
import Icon from "./Icon.astro";
import { RELEASE_URL } from "./links";
---

<section class="sec" id="install">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="rv">
      <div class="label">Local first</div>
      <h2 class="h2">Your code stays on your machine.</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="priv rv">
      <div>
        <h3>Your tools, your sign-ins</h3>
        <p>
          Otaq runs the real <span class="mono">claude</span>, <span class="mono">codex</span>,{" "}
          <span class="mono">copilot</span> and <span class="mono">opencode</span> programs with your own
          subscriptions and permission modes. Never your passwords or keys.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>No account, no server</h3>
        <p>
          Tickets live in <span class="mono">~/.otaq</span>. Worktrees sit outside your repository, one per
          ticket. Pushes happen only when your flow’s delivery opens a pull request.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Nothing about you leaves</h3>
        <p>
          Once a day Otaq asks GitHub for the newest version, and sends nothing with that request. Anonymous
          usage data is sent only if you opt in: which features are used, the Otaq version and your OS. Never
          code, ticket text or file names.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="status rv">
      <div>
        <h3>
          <span class="pill p-pa">
            <Icon icon="info" />
            v0.1
          </span>
          Download
        </h3>
        <p>
          One setup file per system: macOS (Apple silicon and Intel), Windows 10/11, and Linux as an AppImage
          or a .deb. Otaq updates itself. You need git and at least one agent tool installed and signed in.
        </p>
        <div class="cta" style="margin-top:14px;justify-content:flex-start">
          <a class="btn btn-p" href={RELEASE_URL}>
            <Icon icon="download" />
            Download the latest release
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Honest status</h3>
        <p>
          Otaq is early and used daily by its author. macOS is the most tested; Windows and Linux have seen
          less use. Otaq isn’t code signed, so your system asks you to allow it the first time you open it.
          Expect rough edges, and please open issues.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
